            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Charlie Jackson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "As a boy in NY, I grew up on these. In my neighborhood, they used ice-cold milk, and whipped it with the syrup in a blender before adding it to the seltzer water. My favorite was the Vanilla Egg Cream (egg creams were not necessarily Chocolate). Today, I make them with the Torani Vanilla (-not- French Vanilla) syrup."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "jackstraw22"
            },
            "reviewBody": "First, you have to use Fox's U-Bet Chocolate Syrup. Start by pouring a little less than 2 oz. of whole milk(has to be whole milk) into a soda glass. Then add seltzer to fill the glass 1/3 of the way. Stir the milk and seltzer. Then add more seltzer until it reaches the very top of the glass. Then pour 2 oz. of chocolate syrup down the side of the glass. Only stir the bottom of the glass where the chocolate is sitting. Stir until a foamy head appears and the drink has turned brown. And it tastes nothing like a fizzy chocolate milk. I hate chocolate milk but love egg creams.",
            "datePublished": "2012-06-22T09:15:26.197Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "cakelover"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did not grow up on these but still live them. I came across the recipe and wanted to try. I live the fact that it is a lighter version of chocolate milk a great summer drink. I use club soda Hershey’s chocolate syrup and almond milk and it tastes amazing. Recommend 100 percent",
            "datePublished": "2021-07-17T19:27:53.733Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SassyPot9084"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I make them with many flavored variations, using the Torani flavors. Peach, peppermint, raspberry etc. I’m just not a chocolate lover. They’re all great!",
            "datePublished": "2024-01-18T00:02:29.003Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Ginny"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Born raised queens, eggcreams the best! I use orange seltzer, chocolate syrup, milk!",
            "datePublished": "2017-11-06T07:45:44.587Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Paul Becker"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Never had an egg cream. Tried this one and I love it. I used store brand club soda and chocolate sauce. Made a coffee version using Autocrat coffee syrup which was also great.",
            "datePublished": "2014-10-18T13:11:40.470Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "2"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Roxie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Chocolate Egg Creams were made exclusively with Fox's U-Bet chocolate syrup. Fox's has a flavor which is markedly different from any other brand, and is still being created in Brooklyn, NY. Once you've tried it, you won't go back, I promise! Coffee, and Vanilla Egg Creams were also available at the soda fountains. The good old days! This recipe uses far too much milk and not nearly enough seltzer in my opinion.",
            "datePublished": "2012-04-27T05:24:37.963Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Denise Davis"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is great! The only change I made was using light cream or half-n-half. and club soda. I mixed the cream and chocolate syrup in glass, added ice to top and poured the club soda on top. Unbelievably refreshing. It's not bad with a splash of Kahlua too.",
